from lib import *
D="d3"

L("3-01",D,"d3c1","Routing Fundamentals and the Routing Table",["3.1","3.2"],40,
"A router decides where to send each packet by looking up the destination IP address in its routing table. The table lists known networks, how the router learned them, and which next hop or interface to use.",
[(B,"How a router forwards","For each packet, the router removes the Layer 2 frame, reads the destination IP, finds the best matching route, decrements the TTL, builds a new frame for the outgoing interface (with the next hop's MAC), and sends it. If no route matches and there is no default route, the packet is dropped."),
 (I,"Reading 'show ip route'","Codes: C = connected, L = local (the router's own /32 interface address), S = static, O = OSPF, S* = static default route (candidate default). Example: 'O 10.2.2.0/24 [110/2] via 10.0.0.2, 00:05:12, GigabitEthernet0/1'. Components: route source, destination prefix and mask, [administrative distance/metric], next hop, route age, and outgoing interface. 'Gateway of last resort' shows the default route."),
 (X,"Choosing the best route","1) Longest prefix match always wins: for 10.1.1.5, a /24 route beats a /16 beats a /0. 2) If the same prefix is learned from different sources, the lowest administrative distance (AD) wins: connected 0, static 1, eBGP 20, EIGRP 90, OSPF 110, IS-IS 115, RIP 120, iBGP 200, unusable 255. 3) If the same prefix comes from the same protocol, the lowest metric wins (OSPF metric = cost). Equal-cost paths can be load-shared (ECMP). Remember: AD and metric are only compared between routes to the exact same prefix.")],
"Reading a routing table correctly is the single most important IP Connectivity skill and a core exam topic (25% domain).",
[("Longest match","Routes: 10.0.0.0/8 via R2, 10.1.0.0/16 via R3, 10.1.1.0/24 via R4. Packet to 10.1.1.9 → R4. Packet to 10.1.2.9 → R3. Packet to 10.9.9.9 → R2."),
 ("AD decision","10.5.5.0/24 is learned by OSPF (AD 110) and configured as static (AD 1). The static route is installed.")],
["Comparing AD between routes with different prefix lengths (longest match is evaluated first).",
 "Thinking the router chooses the lowest metric across different protocols.",
 "Forgetting about the local (L) /32 routes added for each interface IP."],
[("Administrative distance","Trustworthiness of a route source; lower is preferred."),
 ("Metric","Protocol-specific cost used to choose among routes from the same protocol."),
 ("Longest prefix match","Selecting the route with the most matching network bits."),
 ("Gateway of last resort","The default route used when no more specific route exists."),
 ("ECMP","Equal-cost multipath: load-sharing over equal-metric routes.")],
["Decision order: longest prefix → lowest AD → lowest metric.",
 "AD: connected 0, static 1, eBGP 20, EIGRP 90, OSPF 110, IS-IS 115, RIP 120, iBGP 200.",
 "Route codes C, L, S, S*, O and the [AD/metric] format."],
["Many questions show a routing table and a destination IP and ask for the next hop or exit interface.",
 "Know what happens when two routes have the same prefix but different AD."],
"Routers forward packets using the routing table: longest prefix match first, then lowest administrative distance, then lowest metric.",
[("Which route is used for 172.16.5.10? 172.16.0.0/16 [110/20], 172.16.5.0/24 [1/0], 0.0.0.0/0 [1/0]",["172.16.0.0/16","172.16.5.0/24","0.0.0.0/0","Load-balanced"],1,"/24 is the longest prefix match."),
 ("What is the default administrative distance of OSPF?",["90","100","110","120"],2,"OSPF = 110."),
 ("In '[110/3]', what is the 3?",["Hop count","Administrative distance","Metric","Route age"],2,"Format is [AD/metric].")],
diagrams=["routing"])

L("3-02",D,"d3c1","Static Routing for IPv4 and IPv6",["3.3"],35,
"A static route is a route you type in manually. It is simple, predictable, and uses no bandwidth for routing updates, but it does not adapt automatically when the network changes.",
[(B,"Basic syntax","'ip route <destination network> <mask> <next-hop IP | exit interface>'. Example: ip route 192.168.20.0 255.255.255.0 10.0.0.2. The route appears in the table as 'S' with AD 1."),
 (I,"Types of static routes","Network route: a specific subnet. Default route: 'ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 203.0.113.1' matches everything (S*). Host route: a /32 such as 'ip route 10.9.9.9 255.255.255.255 10.0.0.2'. Floating static route: a backup route with an AD set higher than the primary, e.g., 'ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 198.51.100.1 130' stays out of the table while an OSPF-learned default (AD 110) exists."),
 (X,"IPv6 static routes and next-hop choices","Enable 'ipv6 unicast-routing'. 'ipv6 route 2001:DB8:2::/64 2001:DB8:12::2', default 'ipv6 route ::/0 GigabitEthernet0/1 FE80::2'. When the next hop is a link-local address, you must specify the exit interface as well. Using only an exit interface on an Ethernet (multi-access) link is discouraged for IPv4 because the router relies on proxy ARP; prefer next-hop or fully specified routes. A static route is only installed if its next hop is reachable (or exit interface is up). Verify with 'show ip route static' and 'show ipv6 route static'.")],
"Static routes are used at network edges (default route to an ISP), in stub branches, and as backups to dynamic routing.",
[("Branch default route","ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 10.255.0.1 sends all non-local traffic to HQ."),
 ("Floating backup","Primary default learned via OSPF (AD 110); backup 'ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 172.16.99.1 200' takes over only if OSPF's route disappears.")],
["Setting a floating route AD lower than the primary, so it becomes the primary.",
 "Using a link-local next hop without an exit interface in IPv6.",
 "Forgetting the return route on the remote router (traffic goes out but replies cannot come back)."],
[("Static route","Manually configured route; AD 1 by default."),
 ("Default route","0.0.0.0/0 or ::/0; matches any destination."),
 ("Floating static route","Backup static route with raised AD."),
 ("Fully specified route","Static route with both exit interface and next-hop IP.")],
["ip route NET MASK NEXTHOP [AD]; ipv6 route PREFIX/LEN [INTERFACE] NEXTHOP [AD].",
 "Default: 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 / ::/0. Host route: /32 or /128.",
 "Floating route AD must be higher than the primary route's AD.",
 "IPv6 link-local next hop requires an exit interface."],
["Expect to pick the correct floating static AD value given an OSPF or EIGRP primary."],
"Static routes provide manual, predictable paths: network, host, default, and floating routes for IPv4 and IPv6, with floating routes acting as backups via higher AD.",
[("Which command creates a floating static default route backing up OSPF?",["ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 10.1.1.1 1","ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 10.1.1.1 100","ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 10.1.1.1 115","ip route 0.0.0.0 255.255.255.255 10.1.1.1"],2,"AD must be greater than 110; 115 works."),
 ("Which IPv6 static route is valid with a link-local next hop?",["ipv6 route ::/0 FE80::1","ipv6 route ::/0 Gi0/0 FE80::1","ip route ::/0 FE80::1","ipv6 route 0/0 FE80::1"],1,"A link-local next hop requires the exit interface."),
 ("What is the default AD of a static route?",["0","1","5","110"],1,"Static = 1.")],
diagrams=["routing"])

L("3-03",D,"d3c2","OSPFv2 Single Area",["3.4"],50,
"OSPF is a link-state routing protocol. Routers form neighbor relationships, share information about their links, build an identical map of the network, and use Dijkstra's SPF algorithm to calculate the shortest path to every network.",
[(B,"Link-state in plain terms","Each OSPF router describes its links in Link-State Advertisements (LSAs). All routers in an area store these LSAs in the link-state database (LSDB). Every router independently runs SPF on the same LSDB to build its routing table. Cost is the metric: lower is better."),
 (I,"Neighbors and adjacencies","Routers send Hellos to 224.0.0.5 every 10 seconds (dead interval 40 s on broadcast and point-to-point networks). Neighbor states: Down → Init (Hello received) → 2-Way (saw own RID in neighbor's Hello) → ExStart (master/slave chosen) → Exchange (DBDs exchanged) → Loading (LSRs/LSUs) → Full. To become neighbors, routers must match: area ID, subnet/mask, Hello and dead timers, authentication, and stub flag; they need unique router IDs, and MTU must match to reach Full."),
 (X,"Network types, DR/BDR, router ID, cost, and configuration","On broadcast networks (Ethernet default), a DR and BDR are elected to reduce flooding: highest interface priority (default 1; 0 = never), then highest router ID. The election is non-preemptive. DROTHERs reach Full only with the DR and BDR and stay 2-Way with each other; updates to DR/BDR use 224.0.0.6. On point-to-point networks ('ip ospf network point-to-point'), no DR/BDR is elected. Router ID: manually set ('router-id 1.1.1.1'), else highest loopback IP, else highest active physical interface IP. Cost = reference bandwidth (default 100 Mbps) / interface bandwidth; change with 'auto-cost reference-bandwidth 10000' (in Mbps) or 'ip ospf cost'. Configure: router ospf 1 / network 10.0.12.0 0.0.0.3 area 0 (wildcard mask) or interface-level 'ip ospf 1 area 0'; 'passive-interface Gi0/2' (stop Hellos on user LANs while still advertising them); 'default-information originate' to advertise a default route. Verify: show ip ospf neighbor, show ip ospf interface [brief], show ip protocols, show ip route ospf.")],
"OSPF is the most widely used enterprise interior routing protocol and the only dynamic routing protocol you configure on the CCNA.",
[("Two routers on a /30","R1 and R2 on 10.0.12.0/30, area 0, network type point-to-point. Both show FULL/ - with no DR/BDR."),
 ("Adjacency failure","R1 Hello 10/Dead 40, R2 Hello 5/Dead 20 → they never become neighbors. 'show ip ospf interface' reveals the timer mismatch."),
 ("DR election","Three routers on a LAN; R3 has priority 255 → R3 is DR (if it is up when the election runs).")],
["Using a subnet mask instead of a wildcard mask in the 'network' command.",
 "Expecting OSPF process IDs to match between neighbors (they are locally significant).",
 "Expecting DROTHERs to be FULL with each other.",
 "Forgetting that DR election does not preempt; raising priority later has no effect until a re-election."],
[("LSA / LSDB","Link-State Advertisement / Link-State Database."),
 ("SPF","Shortest Path First (Dijkstra) algorithm."),
 ("Router ID","32-bit dotted-decimal ID unique to each OSPF router."),
 ("DR / BDR","Designated Router / Backup DR on multi-access networks."),
 ("Passive interface","Advertised network on which no Hellos are sent."),
 ("Area 0","Backbone area; single-area OSPF uses only area 0 in most designs.")],
["Hello 10 s, Dead 40 s (broadcast/P2P). AD 110. Multicast 224.0.0.5 (all) and 224.0.0.6 (DR/BDR).",
 "States: Down, Init, 2-Way, ExStart, Exchange, Loading, Full.",
 "Neighbor must-match: area, subnet, timers, authentication, stub flag; unique RID; MTU for Full.",
 "RID order: manual → highest loopback → highest active interface.",
 "Cost = 100 Mbps / bandwidth (default reference)."],
["'show ip ospf neighbor' output reading is heavily tested: FULL/DR, FULL/BDR, 2WAY/DROTHER, FULL/ - (point-to-point).",
 "Process ID is local; area must match."],
"OSPFv2 forms adjacencies using Hellos, synchronizes LSDBs, and computes shortest paths with SPF using cost. Know neighbor requirements, states, DR/BDR election, router ID selection, and configuration/verification commands.",
[("Which value must match for two OSPF routers to become neighbors?",["Process ID","Router ID","Area ID","Interface priority"],2,"Area ID must match; process IDs are locally significant and RIDs must be unique."),
 ("Two DROTHERs on the same LAN will settle in which state?",["Full","2-Way","ExStart","Init"],1,"DROTHERs remain 2-Way with each other."),
 ("With default reference bandwidth, what is the cost of a FastEthernet (100 Mbps) interface?",["1","10","19","100"],0,"100/100 = 1. (Gigabit is also 1, which is why the reference is often raised.)"),
 ("Which command stops OSPF Hellos on a LAN interface but still advertises its subnet?",["no network","passive-interface","ip ospf priority 0","shutdown"],1,"passive-interface.")],
diagrams=["ospf_states"])

L("3-04",D,"d3c3","First Hop Redundancy Protocols",["3.5"],25,
"Hosts usually have only one default gateway configured. If that router fails, they lose access to other networks. A First Hop Redundancy Protocol (FHRP) lets two or more routers share a virtual IP that hosts use as their gateway, so another router takes over automatically.",
[(B,"Virtual gateway","Routers R1 (10.1.1.2) and R2 (10.1.1.3) share virtual IP 10.1.1.1 and a virtual MAC. Hosts use 10.1.1.1. One router actively forwards; the other waits."),
 (I,"HSRP, VRRP, GLBP","HSRP (Cisco): active/standby routers; default priority 100 (higher wins); preemption disabled by default; virtual MAC 0000.0C07.ACxx (v1) or 0000.0C9F.Fxxx (v2); Hellos to 224.0.0.2 (v1) or 224.0.0.102 (v2). VRRP (IEEE/IETF standard, RFC 5798): master/backup; virtual MAC 0000.5E00.01xx; multicast 224.0.0.18; preemption enabled by default. GLBP (Cisco): one AVG answers ARP with different virtual MACs so multiple AVFs forward simultaneously (load balancing)."),
 (X,"What the CCNA expects","Objective 3.5 is 'Describe the purpose, functions, and concepts of first hop redundancy protocols'. Be able to explain the virtual IP/MAC concept, failover, and differences between HSRP, VRRP, and GLBP. Basic HSRP syntax for reference: interface Gi0/0 / standby 1 ip 10.1.1.1 / standby 1 priority 110 / standby 1 preempt; verify with 'show standby brief'. When failover occurs, the new active router sends gratuitous ARPs so switches update their MAC tables.")],
"Gateway failure would otherwise isolate an entire VLAN. FHRPs provide sub-second to few-second failover transparent to hosts.",
[("Campus VLAN 10","Two distribution switches run HSRP for VLAN 10 with virtual IP 10.10.10.1; the left switch is active with priority 110 and preempt."),
 ("Standards-based","A multi-vendor site uses VRRP because HSRP and GLBP are Cisco proprietary.")],
["Configuring the virtual IP as one router's physical IP.",
 "Assuming preemption is enabled by default in HSRP.",
 "Thinking HSRP load-balances per host (that is GLBP)."],
[("Virtual IP","Shared gateway address configured on hosts."),
 ("Active / Standby","HSRP roles."),
 ("Master / Backup","VRRP roles."),
 ("AVG / AVF","GLBP Active Virtual Gateway / Active Virtual Forwarder."),
 ("Preemption","Allows a higher-priority router to take over the active role.")],
["HSRP: Cisco, active/standby, priority 100, no preempt by default, 0000.0C07.ACxx (v1).",
 "VRRP: standard, master/backup, 0000.5E00.01xx, 224.0.0.18.",
 "GLBP: Cisco, load balancing with AVG/AVFs."],
["v1.1 asks you to describe FHRPs (not configure). Focus on concepts and comparisons."],
"FHRPs provide a resilient default gateway using a shared virtual IP and MAC. HSRP and GLBP are Cisco proprietary; VRRP is an open standard; GLBP adds load balancing.",
[("Which FHRP is an open standard?",["HSRP","GLBP","VRRP","CARP"],2,"VRRP is defined by the IETF."),
 ("What is the default HSRP priority?",["0","1","100","255"],2,"Default 100; higher wins."),
 ("Which FHRP natively load-balances across multiple gateways?",["HSRP","VRRP","GLBP","OSPF"],2,"GLBP uses multiple AVFs.")],
diagrams=["fhrp"])
